{"name":"Ice Rink","hex":"#bbddee","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"ice rink","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A specially frozen surface of water on which people skate or play ice hockey; the building that houses such a surface."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ice_rink"}],"entries":[{"title":"Ice rink","blocks":[{"text":"An ice rink is a frozen body of water or an artificial sheet of ice where people can ice skate or play winter sports. Rinks are also used for exhibitions, contests and ice shows. The growth and increasing popularity of skating during the 1800s marked a rise in the deliberate construction of rinks in numerous areas of the world."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/Rockefeller-ice.jpg/330px-Rockefeller-ice.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ice_rink","wikidata":"Q1282870"},{"title":"Ice","blocks":[{"text":"Ice is water that is frozen into a solid state, typically forming at or below temperatures of 0 °C, 32 °F, or 273.15 K. It occurs naturally on Earth, on other planets, in Oort cloud objects, and as interstellar ice. As a naturally occurring crystalline inorganic solid with an ordered structure, ice is considered to be a mineral. Depending on the presence of impurities such as particles of soil or bubbles of air, it can appear transparent or a more or less opaque bluish-white color."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/Ice_Block%2C_Canal_Park%2C_Duluth_%2832752478892%29.jpg/330px-Ice_Block%2C_Canal_Park%2C_Duluth_%2832752478892%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ice","wikidata":"Q23392"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/Z_zabawy_na_lodzie_odbytej_d._26_lutego_r._b._-_Rysowa%C5%82_C._Jankowski_%2859016%29.jpg/500px-Z_zabawy_na_lodzie_odb